  "passages": {
    "2d69b5a05f60478bd41cc41af42bb0": {
      "source_id": "US-UT:commencement:6b08396dfa72",
      "citation": "Utah R. Civ. P. 3(a)",
      "authority_type": "court_rule",
      "language": "en",
      "text": "(a) How commenced. A civil action is commenced (1) by filing a complaint with the court, or (2) by service of a summons together with a copy of the complaint in accordance with Rule 4. If the action is commenced by the service of a summons and a copy of the complaint, then the complaint, the summons and proof of service, must be filed within ten days of such service. If, in a case commenced under paragraph (a)(2) of this rule, the complaint, summons and proof of service are not filed within ten days of service, the action commenced shall be deemed dismissed and the court shall have no further jurisdiction thereof. If a check or other form of payment tendered as a filing fee is dishonored, the party shall pay the fee by cash or cashier's check within 10 days after notification by the court. Dishonor of a check or other form of payment does not affect the validity of the filing, but may be grounds for such sanctions as the court deems appropriate, which may include dismissal of the action and the award of costs and attorney fees.",
      "text_sha256": "e859739b6d3a2c570dc91ac7ee6e2b95390f21c28d012dbaf7f32abf8687075a",
      "publisher_sha256": "7cc4be87525e9869fb2c7d4b635d667aa3010723ed187050e3c0f7043a02b9b8",
      "retrieved_at": "2026-11-17T20:14:06.970687+00:00",
      "currentness": "unresolved"
    },
    "6e1134ec6760a157c37ef7bc0e8f05": {
      "citation": "Utah Code § 78B-5-818",
      "source_id": "US-UT:fault:106d58d09095",
      "authority_type": "statute",
      "language": "en",
      "text": "78B-5-818 Comparative negligence. (1) The fault of a person seeking recovery may not alone bar recovery by that person. (2) A person seeking recovery may recover from any defendant or group of defendants whose fault, combined with the fault of persons immune from suit and nonparties to whom fault is allocated, exceeds the fault of the person seeking recovery prior to any reallocation of fault made under Subsection 78B-5-819(2). (3) No defendant is liable to any person seeking recovery for any amount in excess of the proportion of fault attributed to that defendant under Section 78B-5-819. (4) (a) The fact finder may, and when requested by a party shall, allocate the percentage or proportion of fault attributable to each person seeking recovery, to each defendant, to any person immune from suit, and to any other person identified under Subsection 78B-5-821(4) for whom there is a factual and legal basis to allocate fault. In the case of a motor vehicle accident involving an unidentified motor vehicle, the existence of the vehicle shall be proven by clear and convincing evidence which may consist solely of one person's testimony. (b) Any fault allocated to a person immune from suit is considered only to accurately determine the fault of the person seeking recovery and a defendant and may not subject the person immune from suit to any liability, based on the allocation of fault, in this or any other action.",
      "text_sha256": "12bcba4d7dd8aa5e1b46040471ed4de631a801ba4471cd8001edcef435a8f7b1",
      "publisher_sha256": "d319d63f4c92ec50110872bf997470d21359c66f431f6580c8946e2da0b0f5c8",
      "retrieved_at": "2026-11-17T20:50:36.057708+00:00",
      "currentness": "unresolved"
    },
    "6977b28f113b4e78de464a00fecc1f": {
      "source_id": "US-UT:contract_formation:92d7342ac769",
      "authority_type": "statute",
      "citation": "Utah Code § 70A-2-204",
      "text": "A contract for sale of goods may be made in any manner sufficient to show agreement, including conduct by both parties which recognizes the existence of such a contract.",
      "text_sha256": "9c77be9f087223530e0d86a71047d66373e3a4889718dffe0aff6b1ded1679b9",
      "publisher_sha256": "159729e5c447c8dc0de674de36674d318bb451b6859afd2be39bed6bcf606b43",
      "retrieved_at": "2026-11-18T15:14:33.702908+00:00",
      "currentness": "unresolved",
      "language": "en"
    },
    "0b96aaa65d931279b26028a62c5edc": {
      "source_id": "US-UT:service:50fe939bd0bb",
      "authority_type": "court_rule",
      "citation": "Utah R. Civ. P. 4(d)(1) (personal service)",
      "text": "(1) Personal service. The summons and complaint may be served by any person 18 years of age or older at the time of service and not a party to the action or a party's attorney. If the person to be served refuses to accept a copy of the summons and complaint, service is sufficient if the person serving them states the name of the process and offers to deliver them.",
      "text_sha256": "83c2156d7f9102724858c96d68ef0bea7a41caa00575b074a15d3e89ef0463ed",
      "publisher_sha256": "7cc4be87525e9869fb2c7d4b635d667aa3010723ed187050e3c0f7043a02b9b8",
      "retrieved_at": "2026-11-17T20:14:06.970687+00:00",
      "currentness": "unresolved",
      "language": "en"
    },
    "0a5514f7f922e54b7390860b3f667e": {
      "source_id": "US-UT:contract_damages:f60d29c5c816",
      "authority_type": "statute",
      "citation": "Utah Code § 70A-2-714",
      "text": "Where the buyer has accepted goods and given notification (Subsection (3) of Section 70A-2-607) he may recover as damages for any nonconformity of tender the loss resulting in the ordinary course of events from the seller's breach as determined in any manner which is reasonable. (2) The measure of damages for breach of warranty is the difference at the time and place of acceptance between the value of the goods accepted and the value they would have had if they had been as warranted, unless special circumstances show proximate damages of a different amount.",
      "text_sha256": "10733b4a1bf8d131b7f3e60dcdd8a727063ff8d50d9ca659f8b179fed68b5eb9",
      "publisher_sha256": "159729e5c447c8dc0de674de36674d318bb451b6859afd2be39bed6bcf606b43",
      "retrieved_at": "2026-11-18T15:14:33.702908+00:00",
      "currentness": "unresolved",
      "language": "en"
    },
    "0e477d509c2be785e46f468635795f": {
      "source_id": "US-UT:consumerLaw:36784f3b7143",
      "authority_type": "statute",
      "citation": "Utah Code § 13-11-4(1)",
      "text": "(1) A supplier that engages in a deceptive act or practice in connection with a consumer transaction violates this chapter, whether the deceptive act or practice occurs before, during, or after the transaction.",
      "text_sha256": "95cbfb551e128044ed6343a4a6ab91b854efb9d84b52910611a69ae2b5c0d89a",
      "publisher_sha256": "12f11ba7d0df24246b2d87cfbf73d13f74773dbec2d8eea85916beb97787391b",
